course: medicine
lesson: 53
title: Breakfast before a match and food after
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Breakfast literally breaks a fast: if you ate tea at 6 pm and play at 10 am, your body has gone 16 hours with no food and needs fuel before it can run.
explain: Matilda's dad makes porridge with banana at 8:30 for an 11:00 match, «No, love, gives it time to go down», because a proper meal about 2 to 3 hours before sport leaves the stomach time to empty.
explain: Short on time, like Dylan with 40 minutes to kick-off, «Mardy breakfast, Mam» («mardy» is Midlands for sulky)? A small light snack such as a banana is far better than nothing, and far better than a greasy fry-up.
explain: After the final whistle your muscles need repairing and refuelling, so a proper meal or snack with starchy food and some protein, like a sandwich, pasta, yoghurt or a glass of milk, is part of the training.
fact: After a night's sleep the body needs fuel; skipping breakfast before morning sport can leave a player shaky, slow and unable to focus.
fact: A proper meal is best eaten about 2 to 3 hours before sport so the stomach has time to empty.
fact: Porridge, toast, cereal and fruit give steady energy before sport.
fact: If time is short, a small easy snack like a banana or a slice of toast gives some fuel without a heavy stomach.
fact: Fatty, heavy food like a big fry-up takes a long time to digest, and running on it can cause tummy ache, burping and feeling sick.
fact: For very early training, a good tea the night before plus a light snack and a drink on waking works well.
fact: Half-time orange slices are a tradition, but a sensible one: a little water and natural sugar in a small, light amount.
fact: After sport the body needs to refuel and repair; carbs and some protein, such as a sandwich, pasta, yoghurt or milk, help recovery.
fact: Crisps are mostly salt and fat and are not enough to recover after sport.
fact: Milk gives fluid, protein for muscles and calcium for bones, which makes it a good drink after exercise.
fact: One treat meal to celebrate is fine, but sport doesn't cancel out a huge pile of junk food.
fact: Many sports canteens sell mostly chocolate and fizzy drinks, so for a long tournament it is wise to pack sandwiches, fruit and water.
fact: A good tea and a good night's sleep after sport let muscles repair and refill energy for the next day.
fact: «Beta» is a Hindi and Urdu word for son or child, and «full of beans» means full of energy.
exercise: Why does breakfast matter before morning sport? | The body needs fuel after the night | It makes you lighter and faster | Sport is better on an empty stomach | It stops you needing water
exercise: Tea at 6 pm, match at 10 am. How long without food if you skip breakfast? | 16 hours | 4 hours | 10 hours | 24 hours
exercise: When is a proper meal best eaten before sport? | About 2 to 3 hours before | Right before kick-off, so the food is fresh | During the warm-up, a bite at a time | Only the night before, never on the day
exercise: Which is a good breakfast before a match? | Porridge with banana | Sausages, bacon and fried bread | A family bar of chocolate | Nothing at all
exercise: Kick-off in 40 minutes and you're starving. What's best? | A small light snack like a banana | A huge fry-up | Nothing until after | A big bowl of chips
exercise: Why can a greasy fry-up before sport make you feel sick? | Fatty food takes a long time to digest | It has no energy in it at all, so it's wasted | It cools the stomach | It is too salty to swallow
exercise: Swimming training at 6 am. How do you fuel up? | A good tea the night before and a light snack | Nothing; early training needs no fuel at all, only water | A big fry-up at 5:45, just before getting in the pool | An energy drink on waking and nothing else until lunch
exercise: Are half-time orange slices sensible? | Yes, a little water and sugar in a light amount | No, fruit at half-time is dangerous and gives you cramp | Only if they are sugar-coated | No, only sweets give energy
exercise: What does the body need soon after sport? | Food with carbs and some protein | Only a bag of crisps, because the salt replaces sweat | Nothing until bedtime | Only a fizzy drink
exercise: Why is milk a good drink after sport? | It gives fluid, protein and calcium | It's only for babies, so big kids get nothing from it | It cools the muscles down faster than water does | It has lots of caffeine to wake you up again
exercise: Is one treat meal after a big win fine? | Yes, one treat, not the whole menu | No, never any treats | Yes, eat the whole menu | Only if you scored
exercise: Long tournament, the canteen sells only sweets. What should you have done? | Packed sandwiches, fruit and water | Brought more money so he could buy extra sweets | Eaten nothing all day so he'd feel light and quick | Brought energy drinks to get him through the day
exercise: Why eat a good tea after sport? | To repair muscles and refuel for tomorrow | So you can skip breakfast the next morning instead | To make you sleep less | It doesn't matter at all
exercise: What does «full of beans» mean? | Full of energy | Very hungry | Feeling sick | Ate too much tea
test: Harry rolls out of bed at 9:40 for a 10:00 kick-off: «skip breakfast, I'll run on vibes». Second half? | Dizzy and slow | Faster than ever | Exactly the same | Too full to run
test: «Bit early, Dad?» — porridge at 8:30 for an 11:00 match. Why does Dad say it's right? | It gives the food time to go down | It's the only time he can cook | Porridge works only in the morning | It makes her too full to run
test: Arjun's uncle: «proper breakfast for a champion, beta!» — a big greasy fry-up. Why does Arjun feel sick after two overs? | Heavy fatty food sits in the stomach | He didn't eat enough of it | The sausages were too cold | Bowling makes everyone sick
test: Sienna moans: «breakfast at 4 in the morning?» before 6 am training. Coach's plan? | Bigger tea the night before and a light snack | Skip food until lunch, then eat a double portion | Eat a full roast at 4 am | Energy drink in the car
test: Mohammed: «are half-time oranges a real thing or just tradition?» Best answer? | Both; a tradition, but a sensible one | Just tradition; oranges do nothing a glass of water can't | Real, they double your speed | Neither, they're banned now
test: Ellie has crisps after the match, then nothing till 8 pm: «I'm wrecked». What would have helped? | A proper meal or snack soon after | A second bag of crisps | Nothing, hunger makes you stronger | A fizzy drink at 7 pm
test: Finn's dad hands him milk after rugby. «Milk? Am I a baby?» What's true? | Milk is a great recovery drink at any age | Milk is only for toddlers, older kids should drink juice | Milk makes muscles weaker | Milk should only be drunk before sport
test: Jacob: «we won, no cap, I earned the lot» — burger, large fries, shake and sundae. What's sensible? | Celebrate with one treat, not the whole menu | Order it all again, because winners burn it off anyway | Sport cancels out any amount of junk you eat that day | Skip food altogether so there's room for cake later
test: Lucas brings nothing to a 5-hour tournament: «I'll just buy stuff at the canteen, eh». What could go wrong? | The canteen may only sell sweets and fizzy drinks | Canteens are never open | He'll get too much fruit | Nothing, canteens always sell meals
test: Saoirse eats her tea after camogie (an Irish stick-and-ball game) and wakes full of beans. Her brother skipped tea. How does he likely wake? | Crabby and flat | Full of beans too | Faster than her | Not hungry at all
test: What would happen if a player ate a big plate of chips just before running? | It would likely sit heavy and could make him feel sick | He'd run twice as fast | He'd never need to eat again | Nothing, food doesn't affect running
exam: Identify the interval recommended between the consumption of a substantial meal and the commencement of sport. | Approximately two to three hours | Approximately two to three minutes | Approximately two to three days | No interval is required
exam: It is given that kick-off is to take place in forty minutes. Select the most appropriate form of nourishment. | A small and light snack such as a banana | A substantial fried breakfast | No nourishment whatsoever | A large quantity of confectionery
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of food consumed following sport? | It assists the refuelling and repair of the body | It is of no consequence whatsoever | It impairs recovery in all cases | It serves solely to satisfy appetite
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which milk is regarded as a suitable beverage following exercise. | It supplies fluid, protein and calcium | It contains a high concentration of caffeine | It is suitable exclusively for infants | It cools the muscles directly
